# T7-007 -- acquire-fence dup independence (plan T7 corpus, §4.9 / drm-cxx #230)
#
# A buffer's acquire fence says "these pixels are not valid yet". Where the
# assigned plane exposes IN_FENCE_FD the descriptor goes to KMS and the kernel
# holds scanout until it signals, so the layer reaches the screen only after
# the producer's writes have landed.
#
# The property under test is that a source hands out an *independently
# closeable* descriptor every time it is acquired, rather than the fence it
# holds. A source that gave away ownership would arm the first frame and then
# have nothing: the second frame commits, the kernel accepts it, every counter
# balances, and the buffer scans out unsynced. The symptom is a torn frame
# under load and nothing in the report says so.
#
# What the trace shows is IN_FENCE_FD on the apply of *each* fenced frame --
# two consecutive ones here. One would prove nothing; a source that handed
# over its only fence would still manage the first.
#
# Measured while writing this, and worth recording because it contradicts the
# reason given in `two_acquires_yield_independently_closeable_fences_vkms`:
# that case says the scene acquires each source twice per frame, once for the
# TEST_ONLY commit and once for the real one. It does not, on this path. Six
# frames over two layers produce twelve acquires -- exactly one per source per
# frame, including the frame that searches. The test commit is built by
# DeviceCommitter from the allocator's Layer property bags, which never carry
# the fence, so IN_FENCE_FD appears on applies only. Both implementations
# agree on that, byte for byte, which is what makes it a pinned behaviour
# rather than a divergence. The dup still matters -- it is what lets the same
# fence arm frame after frame -- but for that reason, not the stated one.
#
# The fence is a real one: `mint` keeps the out-fence of a frame that has
# already landed and is therefore already signalled. An unsignalled fence
# armed as IN_FENCE_FD would simply hang the flip, and a scenario that hung
# would be worse than none.

add bg  0 0 0 0 0
add ov1 1 64 64 256 256

# Settle first: the interesting frame must be one that searches, and a cold
# start searches for reasons of its own.
frame
frame

# Keep this frame's out-fence. It signals when the frame lands, so by the time
# anything arms it, it is already signalled.
mint

# ov1's buffer now carries a render-done fence.
fence ov1

# A geometry change defeats the FB-only fast path, so this frame searches
# before it applies.
move ov1 80 80
frame

# The frame that proves the dup: the source has been acquired once already and
# must still arm this one.
frame

# And once the producer stops fencing, IN_FENCE_FD stops being written.
unfence ov1
move ov1 64 64
frame
